course: everyday
lesson: 507
title: Siblings as best mates and as rivals
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: In Bristol, Mo, 10, gets told off for being late for school. His sister Hattie, 9, puts her hand up: «It's my fault, Miss, I made him wait for me.» On the way out Mo whispers, «You're lush, ta» (lush means lovely, ta means thanks). In Houston, Tunde, 12, helps his brother Dayo with math every night at the kitchen table, and Dayo, who can draw anything, helps Tunde with his art project. «We're a two-man team, bro.» Some brothers and sisters are best mates who back each other up and share what they're good at.
explain: Others are rivals. In Glasgow, Jago and Lewis race everything: who finishes his cornflakes first, who gets to the bus stop first, who scores more in the back green. «I'm the best,» says Jago. «Shut it, ya bampot,» says Lewis (Scots for idiot). It's a laugh until someone ends up crying. In London, Mum says to Elowen, «Why can't you be more like Rosa?» because Rosa got top marks again. Elowen goes to her room feeling useless. Being compared to a sibling hurts, and she is allowed to tell Mum so: «I'm not Rosa, I'm me.»
explain: On a sheep farm near Brecon, Gwil, 11, wins every game of everything, so Wynne, 9, just stops playing. Gwil notices and starts «accidentally» missing his shots. Wynne spots it at once: «You're letting me win, you mochyn!» (Welsh for pig). Wynne is half insulted and half chuffed that Gwil cares, but a fake win feels like pity. So they try a real handicap instead: Gwil plays football left-footed and gives Wynne a head start in races. Now when Wynne wins, it's a proper win.
explain: Loyalty can go wrong. In Bradford, Hussain cracks Dad's phone screen. Zainab says, «I was there, I pushed him.» She didn't. Now they're both in trouble, and Dad can't work out what really happened. The braver loyalty is standing next to your brother while he tells the truth. In Grimsby a boy calls Pete a loser, and his sister Dilly, a whole head shorter, marches up: «Say that again, I dare yer.» The boy backs off laughing. Pete, red and secretly grateful: «I could've handled it, our Dilly» («our» before a name is how people up North say «my family's»).
explain: Rivals can turn into a team. In Brisbane, Kirra and Jai start a YouTube channel reviewing servo pies (a servo is a petrol station). Every week they row about who edits, until they split the jobs: one films, one edits, and they swap each week. At 50 subscribers they scream, «We're famous, mate!» In Toronto, Bram gets into the rep hockey team (picked from the best players around), and his sister Anouk tells the whole school, «My brother's sick at hockey, eh» (sick means brilliant). Bram goes red, then buys her a box of Timbits, little doughnut balls from Tim Hortons.
fact: Some siblings are best friends who stick up for each other.
fact: Siblings with different strengths can help each other, like maths for art.
fact: Rivalry can make you try harder, but competing to crush someone hurts.
fact: Comparing one child with another («Why can't you be like Rosa?») hurts, and a child may say so calmly.
fact: When a big sibling always wins, the younger one may give up; letting them win on purpose often feels like pity.
fact: A real handicap, like playing with the weaker foot or a head start, makes the game fair and the win real.
fact: Taking the blame for something you didn't do is still a lie, and it makes it harder for parents to be fair.
fact: Loyalty without lying means standing beside your sibling while they tell the truth.
fact: Siblings often defend each other; you can thank them and still say when you'd rather sort it yourself.
fact: Working together on a project, with jobs split fairly, can turn rivals into a team.
fact: Being proud of a sibling's win, and saying so, usually comes back to you.
fact: «Lush» means lovely; «ta» means thanks; «bampot» is Scots for idiot; «mochyn» is Welsh for pig; «servo» is Australian for petrol station; «sick» can mean brilliant.
exercise: Why did Hattie say «It's my fault, Miss»? | Mo was late because she'd made him wait | She wanted to get a detention for fun | Mo had paid her to take the blame | She had forgotten her own homework
exercise: How do Tunde and Dayo help each other? | Tunde helps with math, Dayo with art | They swap chores for money | Tunde does all of Dayo's homework for him | They both copy answers off their older cousin
exercise: What did Lewis call Jago? | A bampot | A legend | A champion | A mochyn
exercise: When does racing everything stop being a laugh? | When someone ends up crying | When one brother wins three times in a row | When the bus finally comes round the corner | When their mum buys a stopwatch for them
exercise: Mum asked Elowen, «Why can't you be more like Rosa?» How did Elowen feel? | Useless | Proud | Excited to beat Rosa | Bored stiff
exercise: Why did Wynne stop playing games with Gwil? | Gwil won every single time | Wynne had too much homework every night | Dad banned games | Wynne preferred to play on his own
exercise: How did Wynne feel when he spotted Gwil missing on purpose? | Half insulted, half chuffed he cared | Delighted, it was a fair win | Scared that Gwil was ill | Angry enough to never play again
exercise: Which is a real handicap that makes a game fair? | Gwil plays left-footed | Gwil misses on purpose when nobody's looking | Gwil lets Wynne take the ball with his hands | Gwil stops scoring once he's three goals up
exercise: Zainab said «I pushed him» when she hadn't. Why was that a problem? | It was a lie and Dad couldn't be fair | Hussain wanted to be blamed on his own | Pushing your brother is always allowed | Dad didn't care about the phone anyway
exercise: What is loyalty without lying? | Standing by him while he tells the truth | Saying you did it, even if you didn't at all | Hiding the broken phone in the garden shed | Blaming the dog so neither of them gets told off
exercise: What did Dilly say to the boy who called Pete a loser? | «Say that again, I dare yer» | «Please be nicer to my big brother» | «I'm telling Miss» | «Pete's not a loser, you're a loser»
exercise: How did Pete feel after Dilly stood up for him? | Embarrassed and grateful | Only angry at his little sister | Totally fine, he didn't notice | Proud of the boy who called him names
exercise: How did Kirra and Jai stop rowing about who edits? | One films, one edits, swap every week | They gave up and deleted the channel | Their mum did all the editing for them | They flipped a coin before every single video
exercise: What is a «servo» in Australia? | A petrol station | A school dinner | A pie shop | A YouTube channel
exercise: What did Anouk mean by «My brother's sick at hockey»? | He's brilliant at it | He gets ill every time he plays | He's terrible at it | He's off the team for being sick
exercise: What did Bram do when he found out Anouk had boasted about him? | Went red and bought her Timbits | Told her off for embarrassing him | Quit the rep team | Pretended she wasn't his sister
test: Gwil, wanting to be nice: «I'll just miss a few on purpose, he won't notice». What's likely? | Wynne will notice and feel pitied | Wynne will never find out | Wynne will feel like a champion | Wynne will want to play every day
test: Kirra films week 1 and Jai edits week 1; they swap every week. Who edits in week 6? | Kirra | Jai | Both of them | Neither, they're done
test: Jago and Lewis raced 10 times, no draws. Jago won 2 more races than Lewis. How many did Lewis win? | 4 | 5 | 6 | 8
test: «Why can't you be more like Rosa? She's top of the class, she is». What would most likely happen if Mum kept saying it? | Elowen would feel worse and might give up | Elowen would get top marks the next week | Rosa and Elowen would become best mates | Nothing at all, comparing never hurts
test: Hussain to Zainab: «Just say u did it, Dad won't shout at u, ur his fave». Best move for Zainab? | Stand next to him while he owns up | Say she did it, Dad will go easy | Hide the phone and say it was stolen | Tell Dad she saw a burglar break it
test: Mo, grinning: «Hattie's me best mate, no cap». Can a sister really be your best mate? | Yes, plenty of siblings are best friends | No, siblings can only ever be rivals | No, best mates must be from school | Only if they're twins
test: A lad at school: «Dilly's tiny, she's all mouth, she couldn't fight a cushion». Pete would rather sort his own problems. What's a kind move for Pete? | Thank Dilly and say he'd rather sort it himself | Tell Dilly she's embarrassing and to go away | Laugh along with the lad about how small she is | Never speak to Dilly at school again
test: In a cartoon, two brothers fight every episode and are never nice to each other. What's true about real siblings? | They can be rivals one minute and best mates the next | Real siblings never fight at all | Real siblings always hate each other | Brothers can't ever be a team
test: «Bram's in the rep team? Big deal, eh, I'm not gonna say anything to him». What usually happens when you cheer a sibling instead? | It usually comes back to you | They get big-headed and hate you | Nothing ever comes back | You lose your own friends
test: Tunde's well good at math and rubbish at drawing; Dayo is the other way round. What's the smartest deal? | Swap help: math for art | Tunde does all of Dayo's homework | Both just copy from the internet | Dayo gives up drawing to do math
test: Jago: «I won the cornflake race, I'm the best at everything». Lewis is crying. What should Jago learn? | Compete for fun, not to crush | Winning at breakfast makes you the best | Crying means Lewis cheated | Racing should be banned for ever
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the voluntary assumption, by one sibling, of blame for an act committed by another? | It constitutes a falsehood and impedes fair judgement | It constitutes an admirable and wholly truthful act | It invariably secures a lighter punishment for both parties | It is required by every family as a matter of loyalty
exam: Identify the arrangement that is most likely to render a contest between an older and a younger sibling fair. | A genuine handicap, such as the weaker foot | The older sibling's secretly deliberate failure to score | The complete abandonment of every competitive game | The award of victory to the younger sibling in advance
exam: Ascertain the most probable consequence of the repeated comparison of one child with a high-achieving sibling. | The child feels diminished and discouraged | The child's achievement immediately exceeds the sibling's | The siblings' affection for one another invariably increases | No consequence whatsoever arises from such comparison
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the division of labour between siblings collaborating upon a project. | Fair division of tasks can render rivals a team | Collaboration between siblings must inevitably fail | Only one sibling ought ever to perform every task | Disagreement proves the project ought to be abandoned
